{
  "dom_id": "step-q95698",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Which of the following probabilities could be approximated using the normal approximation of the binomial distribution?\n\nI. The probability that a fair coin lands on tails $45$ times out of $80$ coin flips.\n\nII. The probability of getting $4$ even numbers on $6$ rolls of a $6$-sided die.\n\nIII. The probability that a fair spinner with four regions, red, green, yellow, and blue, lands on green at least $27$ times out of $60$ spins.",
  "worked_solution": "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have $n=80$ and $p=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =80\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =40 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =80\\cdot (1-0.5) \\\\  & =40 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\n- In situation II, we have $n=6$ and $p=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =6\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =3 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we *cannot* use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\n- In situation III, we have $n=60$ and $p=\\frac{1}{4}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =60\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =15 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =60\\cdot (1-0.25) \\\\  & =45 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
